                <pb facs="0530" n="526" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg526"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1115/3785: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-11-04T19:00:00-05:00">November 4, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>381. Reference Hankow’s November 3, 5 p.m., 30 Americans on Kuling. Please inform
                    Department promptly of outcome of your approach to the Japanese authorities in
                    regard to this matter. Department considers it highly desirable that the
                    American naval commander at Kiukiang be enabled to work out with the Americans
                    on Kuling arrangements for the departure of those who can and will leave and for
                    such assistance to those remaining as may be practicable and appropriate under
                    the circumstances.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking and Hankow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
